{
  "id": "ep149",
  "duration": 40.1,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 5.138,
      "text": "Most people assume a stellar collapse creates an instant cosmic vacuum cleaner.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Hubble Space Telescope- Kepler's Supernova Remnant (visible-light data) (opo0429i).tiff",
        "credit": "NASA, ESA, R. Sankrit and W. Blair (Johns Hopkins University)",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hubble_Space_Telescope-_Kepler%27s_Supernova_Remnant_(visible-light_data)_(opo0429i).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 5.133,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 5.138,
      "end": 10.908,
      "text": "A black hole is indeed so dense that not even light can escape its gravitational grip.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Anatomy of a Black Hole.jpg",
        "credit": "European Southern Observatory - ESO",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anatomy_of_a_Black_Hole.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 5.133,
          "end": 8.233,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 8.233,
          "end": 10.9,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 10.908,
      "end": 16.653,
      "text": "The boundary of no escape is the event horizon, but its reach is surprisingly limited.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "A view of the M87 supermassive black hole in polarised light (eso2105a).jpg",
        "credit": "EHT Collaboration",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_view_of_the_M87_supermassive_black_hole_in_polarised_light_(eso2105a).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 10.9,
          "end": 13.1,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 13.1,
          "end": 16.667,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 16.653,
      "end": 23.348,
      "text": "From a distance, the gravitational field of a black hole is identical to any other body of the same mass.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 16.667,
          "end": 23.333,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 23.348,
      "end": 29.068,
      "text": "If you replaced the Sun with a black hole of equal mass, the physics of gravity wouldn't change.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 23.333,
          "end": 29.067,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 29.068,
      "end": 35.318,
      "text": "While the solar system would grow dark and freezing, the actual path of our planet wouldn't move an inch.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "NH-PlutoByMoonlight-ArtistConcept-20150710.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory/Southwest Research Institute (JHUAPL/SwRI) => http://pluto.jhuapl.edu/Multimedia/Images/Artist-Renderings.php",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NH-PlutoByMoonlight-ArtistConcept-20150710.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 29.067,
          "end": 35.333,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 35.318,
      "end": 39.75,
      "text": "Our planets would stay in their current orbits instead of getting sucked inside.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Hubble Space Telescope- Kepler's Supernova Remnant (visible-light data) (opo0429i).tiff",
        "credit": "NASA, ESA, R. Sankrit and W. Blair (Johns Hopkins University)",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hubble_Space_Telescope-_Kepler%27s_Supernova_Remnant_(visible-light_data)_(opo0429i).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 35.333,
          "end": 40.1,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
